#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "560f6e49-18ff-5174-980c-720bcf8ef38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320449} {:answer/id #uuid "22c091c6-f7b5-5527-b8b1-c873c7d06dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320450} {:answer/id #uuid "957940e7-7f35-58a3-a0e4-daa55621d3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320451} {:answer/id #uuid "68f85aa4-9ff4-5d41-b9a6-047f340b4436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320452} {:answer/id #uuid "d781e2b1-7b73-53f5-aa5e-9598d05ee3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22c091c6-f7b5-5527-b8b1-c873c7d06dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320450} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6da3f2-ab9e-5fc4-b2f0-3c180a33303a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "067c622d-d840-5844-b7eb-4a94820f230e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114272" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b72e74a9-5642-549f-a6b7-b14186c798bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356487} {:answer/id #uuid "c3377ca5-5d04-5053-ae07-1682cc7b7428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0.75)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356488} {:answer/id #uuid "3be695e6-2e3c-5145-99d0-b3a364981f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356489} {:answer/id #uuid "c95df3eb-abe5-5ac0-9e67-12996723f5d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0.75,5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356490} {:answer/id #uuid "81f63339-5dce-556e-b06e-ec107c9eda78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-3)$ and $(0.75,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3be695e6-2e3c-5145-99d0-b3a364981f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356489} :answer-field/id #uuid "7790542e-3984-533b-80e0-96aa511d7397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab3bcfde-03b1-5260-8f80-18e8461b51fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-31225" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/e8f5f537ad63035cac39ea672d4fd2bfff61d97590f79f83277e6654c9c05893.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={h}^{″}(x),$ where the function $h(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $h(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $h(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${h}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={h}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${h}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5).$ Therefore, the function $h(x)$ is concave up on the intervals $(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5e96422-26ae-5396-b76d-ff245e96bcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356494} {:answer/id #uuid "ed071224-8daa-5dbc-b675-93c3bf27608c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5e96422-26ae-5396-b76d-ff245e96bcfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356494} :answer-field/id #uuid "6646c1ae-dd3e-59d7-8786-2633e5de810b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4022d6aa-e7a1-5cc8-b441-dd8b1efe822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4022d6aa-e7a1-5cc8-b441-dd8b1efe822f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356497} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cdef858-6874-5c3e-9c38-59e8a66a0ab3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54fddde1-00a5-5c23-9d33-26479c485ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54fddde1-00a5-5c23-9d33-26479c485ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356499} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c2538b3-2625-57b8-a435-029a36687462" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0866cd13-4086-562d-ae6a-193aa22c57ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0866cd13-4086-562d-ae6a-193aa22c57ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356501} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3a484f-84d0-5670-9864-97137baeee92" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ce94e4f-6bff-561d-bee3-cf3e011e5b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-343146" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/19d9904c5236534ee6b4f176d760268df397f644d4ea7016d1d35c4b5db6e413.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,0),$ and $(2,4).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,),\\,(\\,-2,0\\,),\\,(\\,2,4\\,).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9ea2f30-cd83-5d8a-b908-b17291f4f101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9ea2f30-cd83-5d8a-b908-b17291f4f101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356504} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4dc8f1b-3600-59ca-9f0e-bc7d6ed11f54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0430e05f-f020-5ee7-b611-6f83ba763a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0430e05f-f020-5ee7-b611-6f83ba763a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356506} :answer-field/id #uuid "a61a2655-b56d-5549-