5eee6-2b71-5d97-98f4-e10289d41a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356512} {:answer/id #uuid "2f39a81c-7ecc-555d-883b-6ea67e5464ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356513} {:answer/id #uuid "25ea632d-a340-5ca6-b886-7b32f73133ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356514} {:answer/id #uuid "adfc608a-b83c-551a-ada6-6e3a0a4499de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00b5eee6-2b71-5d97-98f4-e10289d41a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356512} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b2e87f-9136-55d0-8549-3fbe6afd36d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bd473db-7b51-5cec-b077-da970bb7b859" :question/math-academy-id "q-79041" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12x-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =12(1-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12(1+x)(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(1+x)(1-x) & >0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-1) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)<0$ when $-1<x<1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)>0,$ which has the solutions $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
